# Project Architecture

This is a Python project using Pygame for game development.
- Game states are defined in files like `game_state_*.py` and are stored in a dictionary named `game_states` in the `init.py` file.
- Different game states extend the base class `GameState` (from `game_state.py`) and implement various methods related to the state of the game (like `on_key_press_*`, `_add_text_layer`, etc.).
- The graphics are managed by the `GraphicsManager` class in the `graphicsmanager.py` file, which uses a dictionary to store sprites in different layers and groups them by name.
- The `constants` module contains all the game constants (like display size, state names, etc.).
- The project uses type annotations throughout the code to improve readability and ensure correct usage of functions.

## Coding Standards
- Use clear, meaningful variable and function names.
- Follow the PEP8 style guide for Python code.
- Write tests for new features and ensure they pass without error in all supported environments (OS, Pygame version, etc.).
- Document the purpose and behavior of new functions/classes with comments.

# External Dependencies
Our graphics library is Pygame:
- [Pygame documentation](https://www.pygame.org/docs/)
- Pygame version: 2.x (check the latest on [Pypi](https://pypi.org/project/pygame/))